# Summary
Introduced: the commit creates an acknowledged WAL-only durability path for ordinary peer RPCs, but the WAL replay path does not apply the same static peer membership validation that is applied to checkpoint artifacts.
The commit changes production peer RPC durability so ordinary RPCs do not write a full restart-artifact checkpoint before responding; only snapshot RPCs still force that checkpoint. This relies on replaying the WAL after restart. However, the durable Unix-peer startup path calls the peer-policy validation callback on the base restart artifact before WAL replay, then restore_with_wal_file replays WAL records and only validates log/state-machine consistency. The static peer policy validation that rejects retained/applied/cached membership mismatches is not re-run on the WAL-replayed log store. Because static peer reconfiguration is explicitly disabled for configured peer policies, this creates a bypass for membership entries that exist only in the post-checkpoint WAL suffix. A crafted or compromised peer RPC source could persist a membership change in the WAL, receive an acknowledgment under the new WAL-only ordinary-RPC path, and after restart have that membership restored without the configured peer-map validation that an artifact checkpoint would have received.

# Resolution
Fixed in commit edeb57e15cd25452962313e6607bfa472467f304:
https://github.com/justincormack/argmin/commit/edeb57e15cd25452962313e6607bfa472467f304

The durable Unix-peer restore path now replays the WAL suffix into a candidate restart artifact and re-runs the configured peer-policy membership validator before constructing live OpenRaft stores. This applies the same static peer-map checks to WAL-replayed retained membership entries that already applied to the base checkpoint artifact.

Regression coverage was added with `control_plane_openraft_unix_peer_durable_rejects_wal_membership_mismatch`, which stores a peer-policy-compatible base artifact, appends a WAL-only membership entry that does not match the configured peer map, and proves `new_experimental_unix_peer_durable_with_wal` fails closed before serving.

