# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm ordinary peer RPC durability can acknowledge WAL-only state without writing a checkpoint artifact before response.
- [x] Confirm durable Unix-peer startup applies the configured peer-policy validator only to the base checkpoint artifact before WAL replay.
- [x] Demonstrate a WAL suffix can contain a membership entry that the configured peer policy would reject.
- [x] Demonstrate startup with `new_experimental_unix_peer_durable_with_wal` succeeds and restores that WAL-only unauthorized membership entry.
- [x] Confirm control behavior: checkpoint artifact membership mismatches and static peer reconfiguration are rejected, showing this is a WAL-replay-specific bypass.
## Report
Validated the finding as a logical durability/restart policy bypass. The commit sets production peer RPC durability to WAL-only for ordinary RPCs: `crates/argmin-s3/src/main.rs:1943-1949` sets `checkpoint_ordinary_rpc: false`, and `crates/argmin-s3/src/main.rs:2104-2127` checkpoints only when `checkpoint_ordinary_rpc` is true or the frame is a Snapshot, so ordinary AppendEntries/Vote RPC handling can respond without a checkpoint artifact. On durable Unix-peer startup, `new_experimental_unix_peer_durable_inner` passes the static peer-policy validator at `crates/storage/src/control_plane_raft.rs:2975-2980`, but `restore_experimental_raft_durable_artifact` invokes it on the base artifact before WAL replay at `crates/storage/src/control_plane_raft.rs:2781-2784`, then calls `artifact.restore_with_wal_file(...)` at `2784-2789`. `restore_with_wal_file` replays the WAL at `5169-5170` and only runs log/state-machine and cached snapshot consistency checks at `5171-5185`; it does not call `validate_peer_policy_membership`, whose checks for retained log entries, state-machine membership, and cached snapshot membership are at `5285-5316`. Static peer reconfiguration is intended to be disallowed at `3076-3082`.

I added a targeted unit-test PoC `poc_unix_peer_durable_wal_membership_suffix_bypasses_peer_policy_validation` in `crates/storage/src/control_plane_raft.rs:17311-17393`. It creates a configured peer policy for nodes `[1,2]`, stores a checkpoint artifact whose retained membership matches that policy, appends a WAL-only membership entry for `[1]`, first proves that `[1]` is rejected by `policy.validate_configured_membership`, then starts `ControlPlaneRaftAuthority::new_experimental_unix_peer_durable_with_wal`. Startup succeeds, the unauthorized WAL-only membership entry is present in the restored log, and reconstructing an artifact from that restored log shows that re-running `validate_peer_policy_membership` would reject it as `retained log entry does not match configured peer map`.

Dynamic result: `cargo test -p storage poc_unix_peer_durable_wal_membership_suffix_bypasses_peer_policy_validation -- --nocapture` produced `running 1 test ... ok ... 1 passed`. Control result: `cargo test -p storage control_plane_openraft_unix_peer_durable_rejects -- --nocapture` produced 5 passing tests, including rejection of checkpoint retained/applied membership mismatches and static peer reconfiguration. This confirms the difference is specifically WAL-suffix replay after checkpoint validation.

Debugger evidence: non-interactive LLDB breakpoints showed the startup chain: stopped at `control_plane_raft.rs:2783` on `validate_artifact(&artifact)?`, continued to `2784` immediately after validation before WAL handling, then stopped at `ControlPlaneRaftRestartArtifact::restore_with_wal_file` at `5169`, called from `restore_experimental_raft_durable_artifact` at `2785`, via `new_experimental_unix_peer_durable_with_wal`. This confirms peer-policy validation happens before WAL replay and is not re-run afterward. Valgrind/gdb were unavailable in the container; ASan was attempted but Rust stable rejected `-Z sanitizer=address`. No memory-safety crash is expected because this is a logic/policy validation bypass.

